+# Boxing Guard Types & Style Matchups (Queensberry Rules)
+
+**Status:** Combat design reference — Project Uppercut
+**Path:** `docs/design/boxing_guard_matchups.md`
+**Provenance:** Consolidated from three research passes — a general boxing-analysis pass, a game-design pass, and an offensive-profile pass. Where the passes contradicted each other, the resolution is recorded in §9 so future edits don't silently flip a deliberate call.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Core design principle
+
+A guard is not "high defense" or "low defense." It is a **body structure** that protects some lanes while exposing others, changes what offense is available from inside it, and costs resources to hold.
+
+> **A guard protects the interception path, not merely the final target. Punches define both a target region and a travel path; guards define protected planes and exposed lanes.**
+
+Example: an uppercut targets the jaw, but the best interception is usually a low/mid forearm structure that bars the upward channel — not gloves parked at the temples.
+
+Every guard should define:
+
+```text
+guard_type
+protected_zones
+weak_zones
+best_against
+vulnerable_to
+counter_options
+offense_modifiers      # punch speed/availability from inside this guard
+guard_state            # positional sub-states (e.g. cross-arm low vs high stack)
+stamina_cost
+mobility_cost
+visibility_cost
+attribute_scaling      # which fighter attributes the guard's value depends on
+```
+
+Every punch should define:
+
+```text
+target_region
+trajectory
+entry_angle
+range
+guard_intercept_zone
+counter_exposure
+```
+
+## 2. Flavor note (commentary / codex)
+
+These guards exist *because* of Queensberry rules: padded gloves make blocking with the hands and forearms viable. The bare-knuckle era used a completely different upright, arms-extended stance. Usable as a commentary or codex line — it keeps the "arcade boxing with real boxing bones" promise visible.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Guard catalog
+
+Each entry: structure, real-world exemplars (for style-ID commentary), the **offensive profile from inside the guard** (what it lets you throw well vs. what it makes awkward), the defensive profile, counter options, and a sim-notes block.
+
+### 3.1 High Guard (Basic Shell)
+
+**Structure:** Both gloves high near the temples/cheeks, chin tucked, elbows angled in. The amateur and pressure-fighter default.
+**Exemplars:** Winky Wright; Golovkin's variant; standard amateur teaching.
+
+**Offense from this guard**
+- *Strong:* fast jabs and 1-2s (hands already sit in launch position), short lead hooks, tight rear uppercuts off a catch at close range.
+- *Awkward:* looping body shots and wide uppercuts — throwing them collapses the structure and exposes the head; long-arc lead hooks.
+
+**Defends well against:** head jabs, crosses, lead hooks to the head, overhands (partial coverage).
+**Vulnerable to:** uppercuts up the middle (especially if the elbows flare), body hooks / liver shots, body jabs, feints exploiting the visibility loss, angle attacks that turn the static shell.
+**Counters:** catch-and-counter cross, elbow-drop into body counter, step-back reset.
+
+```text
+strong_head_damage_reduction
+moderate_arm_condition_damage
+weak_body_coverage
+moderate_counter_readiness
+small_visibility_penalty
+passivity_risk_if_held_long
+```
+
+### 3.2 Peek-a-Boo
+
+**Structure:** Gloves in front of the lower cheeks/chin — *in front of* the face, not beside it — squared stance, elbows tight, chin buried. Inseparable from constant bob/weave, slips, and explosive entries. A full system, not just a hand position (the Cus D'Amato school).
+**Exemplars:** Mike Tyson, Floyd Patterson.
+
+**Offense from this guard**
+- *Strong:* hooks to head and body, and inside uppercuts generated by slip torque; drilled number-system combinations.
+- *Awkward:* long-range straight punching — the style must close distance to work at all.
+
+**Defends well against:** jabs and crosses (movement plus tight gloves), head hooks, uppercuts up the middle (the gloves already occupy that channel — better than a plain high guard).
+**Vulnerable to:** punches *timed on the entry* — check hooks, and the jab-plus-uppercut timing Douglas used to dismantle Tyson; long-range jabbers who deny entry; body jabs at range. The whole system decays as conditioning fades.
+**Counters:** slip-inside counters, bob-and-weave into hook, explosive body-head sequences.
+
+```text
+strong_all_round_close_defense
+high_stamina_cost
+scales_with: conditioning, head_movement
+punished_for: predictable_entry_rhythm
+```
+
+### 3.3 Philly Shell / Shoulder Roll
+
+**Structure:** Bladed stance; lead shoulder raised to guard the chin, lead arm draped horizontally across the torso, rear glove glued to the rear cheek/chin. Built for deflection and instant counters.
+**Exemplars:** Floyd Mayweather Jr., James Toney.
+
+**Offense from this guard**
+- *Strong:* pull-counter right hand, check hook, flicking up-jab from the hip, shoulder-roll-into-right-hand.
+- *Awkward:* stiff driving power jabs; lead-side power generally starts slower from the low-hanging lead arm. (Elite users still landed lead uppercuts from here — model as a speed/skill penalty, not unavailability; see §9.)
+
+**Defends well against:** rear straights/crosses and overhands (rolled off the shoulder), lead hooks to the head (rear-glove catch), lead-side body hooks (draped forearm shield). Excellent for countering single committed power shots.
+**Vulnerable to:** fast jabs (the lead hand is down), straights to the body, opponent lead uppercuts, hooks around/behind the shoulder line, high-volume combinations that stress the roll rhythm, and **southpaws** — the straight left travels down the open side, which is why shell fighters historically switch to a conventional guard against lefties.
+**Counters:** pull counter, roll-and-return right, check hook, pivot out.
+
+```text
+elite_vs_single_committed_power
+high_counter_readiness
+weak_vs: volume, bodywork, hooks_around_shell, southpaw_straights
+requires_high_ringcraft
+stance_interaction: southpaw_penalty
+```
+
+### 3.4 Cross-Arm Guard
+
+**Structure:** Forearms stacked and crossed horizontally over the face/upper chest, elbows pointed slightly forward. Sturdy, hard to split cleanly, low visibility.
+**Exemplars:** Archie Moore, comeback-era George Foreman; Ken Norton ran a hybrid.
+
+**Offense from this guard**
+- *Strong:* wide clubbing hooks, sweeping overhands, backhand-style jab off the top arm.
+- *Awkward:* quick straight jabs and crosses — the crossed forearms physically slow linear extension.
+
+**Defends well against:** head hooks, overhands, straight head shots, short-range flurries. Center-line uppercuts **when the stack sits at chin height** — the bottom forearm bars the upward channel.
+**Vulnerable to:** bodywork of every kind (the defining hole), uppercuts **when the stack rides high** to cover overhands, angled body hooks wrapping underneath, cumulative arm damage from absorbed volume, and slow counter windows — opponents can pot-shot the body at limited risk.
+**Counters:** absorb-then-counter, smother, slow walk-down, clinch/reset when overwhelmed.
+
+```text
+highest_raw_absorption
+guard_state: low_stack  -> blocks uppercut lane, opens overhand
+guard_state: high_stack -> blocks overhand, splittable by uppercut
+arm_condition_damage_high
+low_counter_speed
+large_visibility_penalty
+good_for: durable_fighters
+```
+
+The two-state model resolves a genuine conflict between source passes — see §9.
+
+### 3.5 Long Guard (Extended Lead)
+
+**Structure:** Lead arm extended toward the opponent's face/shoulder to measure, frame, blind, and jam; rear glove home at the chin. Range control as defense.
+**Exemplars:** Wladimir Klitschko's pawing lead; Hearns at range.
+
+**Offense from this guard**
+- *Strong:* stiff long jabs, straight rights fired behind the frame, pull counters.
+- *Awkward:* short inside hooks and tight uppercuts — the stance lives at extreme range.
+
+**Defends well against:** jabs (the lane is already occupied), hooks thrown from too far out (smothered before the arc builds), rushes and forward pressure (the frame stalls entries); denies full extension on straights by holding distance.
+**Vulnerable to:** **overhands looped over the extended arm** (the classic counter — see §9), lead-hand traps and parries, rights timed over the jab, and body attacks / inside hooks once an opponent slips past the frame.
+**Counters:** frame-and-pivot, jab intercept, step-back right.
+
+```text
+distance_control_multiplier
+best_for: tall_reach_builds
+collapses_at: inside_range
+vulnerable_trajectory: over_the_top
+```
+
+### 3.6 Low Lead Hand / Bait Guard
+
+**Structure:** Lead hand carried low to invite attacks, widen vision, and load counters. Defense is reads, pull-backs, and footwork — not structure.
+**Exemplars:** Ali, Roy Jones Jr., Naseem Hamed.
+
+**Offense from this guard**
+- *Strong:* lead hooks and uppercuts launched from below the eyeline (the hardest punches to see coming), pull-counter rights, up-jabs; total offensive freedom and unobstructed vision.
+- *Awkward:* nothing mechanically — the cost is defensive, not offensive.
+
+**Defends well against:** nothing structurally. Its value is bait — predictable jabs, lunges, and overcommitted straights walk into counters.
+**Vulnerable to:** fast jabs, lead hooks, overhands, quick combinations — essentially everything the moment reflexes lag. The style that ages worst.
+**Counters:** pull counter, slip-jab counter, step-back counter, lead-hook trap.
+
+```text
+counter_window_bonus_large
+exposure_maximum
+gates_on: reflex, timing
+ai_note: dangerous_default_for_low_skill_cpu_or_players
+```
+
+### 3.7 Tight Elbow / Body Guard
+
+**Structure:** Elbows welded to the ribs and centerline; hands moderate to high; body protection prioritized.
+
+**Offense from this guard**
+- *Strong:* short shovel hooks and uppercuts inside; compact pocket combinations.
+- *Awkward:* long straight punching from the compressed frame.
+
+**Defends well against:** body hooks, body jabs/straights, uppercuts (dropped forearms intercept the upward path), inside combination pressure. The clearest working example of the §1 interception rule.
+**Vulnerable to:** head hooks, overhands, high jabs, wide-angle shots that turn the compact frame.
+**Counters:** elbow block, forearm wedge, short counter uppercut, step-in smother.
+
+```text
+strong_vs: bodywork, uppercuts, inside_pressure
+reduced_head_coverage
+reduced_offensive_freedom
+good_vs: pressure_fighters
+```
+
+### 3.8 Ear-Muff / Double Forearm (Emergency Cover)
+
+**Structure:** Both gloves/forearms clamped to the sides of the head — the tighter, survival-mode cousin of the high guard, used to ride out flurries.
+
+**Offense from this guard**
+- *Strong:* effectively nothing while covered; short counters only when the storm breaks or the opponent overthrows.
+- *Awkward:* everything.
+
+**Defends well against:** head hooks, overhands, wild combinations, crowded flurry moments.
+**Vulnerable to:** body jabs (free scoring touches), body hooks, uppercuts splitting underneath, straight body attacks; concedes ring position the whole time it is held.
+**Counters:** cover, wait out, step back, counter the overthrow.
+
+```text
+emergency_survival_state
+scoring_penalty_while_active
+ring_position_loss
+weak_vs: bodywork, uppercuts
+```
+
+### 3.9 Forearm Frame / High Elbow Guard
+
+**Structure:** One or both forearms framed across the opponent's line to control space at close range. An inside-control tool, not a pure block.
+
+**Offense from this guard**
+- *Strong:* short hooks off the frame; rear uppercut while the frame occupies the opponent.
+- *Awkward:* long straights while framing.
+
+**Defends well against:** inside hooks (the frame kills shoulder rotation), head entries, clinch attempts, short flurries.
+**Vulnerable to:** body shots under the framing arm, overhands looped around it, straight shots when the frame is misaligned, uppercuts rising beneath it.
+**Counters:** frame-and-pivot, smother, exit-angle creation, short counter.
+
+```text
+inside_control_tool_not_pure_block
+weak_vs: bodywork, angle_changes
+```
+
+### 3.10 Active Hands / Open Guard
+
+**Structure:** Hands mobile — parrying, catching, touching, probing — rather than holding a fixed shape.
+
+**Offense from this guard**
+- *Strong:* jab variations and parry-and-counter straights; disruption offense that breaks rhythm.
+- *Awkward:* committing full power while the hands are busy defending.
+
+**Defends well against:** jabs, single straights, predictable single shots, range entries (touch control interrupts setups).
+**Vulnerable to:** feints (draws bad reactions), layered combinations (too many threats to parry), hooks around the center parries, body attacks while the hands are high and busy.
+**Counters:** parry-counter, hand trap, touch-and-counter, jab disruption.
+
+```text
+high_skill_active_defense
+strong_vs: predictable_singles
+weak_vs: feints, combination_layering, bodywork
+```
+
+---
+
+## 4. Quick reference: guard strengths and weaknesses
+
+| Guard             | Strong Against                              | Weak Against                                          |
+| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
+| High Guard        | Jabs, crosses, head hooks                   | Uppercuts, body shots, angles                         |
+| Peek-a-Boo        | Jabs, crosses, hooks, mid-line uppercuts    | Check hooks, timed entries, body jabs at range        |
+| Philly Shell      | Crosses, overhands, single power shots      | Fast jabs, hooks around, bodywork, southpaw straights |
+| Cross-Arm         | Head hooks, overhands, flurries             | Body shots, uppercuts*, cumulative arm damage         |
+| Long Guard        | Jabs, rushes, hooks at range                | Overhands, body entries, inside hooks                 |
+| Low Lead Hand     | Predictable jabs, lunges (as bait)          | Fast jabs, hooks, overhands, combinations             |
+| Tight Elbow Guard | Body shots, uppercuts, inside pressure      | Head hooks, overhands, high jabs                      |
+| Ear-Muff Guard    | Head hooks, overhands, wild flurries        | Body shots, uppercuts, straight body attacks          |
+| Forearm Frame     | Inside hooks, clinch entries                | Body shots, overhands, uppercuts underneath           |
+| Active Hands      | Jabs, straights, single shots               | Feints, hooks, combinations, body attacks             |
+
+\* State-dependent — see §3.4 and §9.
+
+## 5. Best punches against each guard
+
+| Opponent Guard    | Better Punch Choices                                                    | Worse Punch Choices                          |
+| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
+| High Guard        | Uppercut, body hook, body jab, angle hook                               | Repeated head jabs, straights into gloves    |
+| Peek-a-Boo        | Check hook, body jab, step-back straight, uppercut timed on the entry   | Lazy straights, predictable single jabs      |
+| Philly Shell      | Fast jab, lead hook around, body straight, overhand, southpaw straight  | Naked rear cross, predictable jab-cross      |
+| Cross-Arm         | Body hook, angled body shot, volume to arms, uppercut vs high stack     | Single head hooks, slow overhands into stack |
+| Long Guard        | Overhand over the frame, body entry, slip-inside hook, angle change     | Long straights down the occupied lane        |
+| Low Lead Hand     | Fast jab, lead hook, overhand, double jab                               | Slow lunging straights (that IS the bait)    |
+| Tight Elbow Guard | High hook, overhand, high jab, angle shot                               | Body spam, unset-up low uppercuts            |
+| Ear-Muff Guard    | Body jab, body hook, uppercut, sustained ring pressure                  | Wild head hooks into the cover               |
+| Forearm Frame     | Body hook, step-around hook, overhand, uppercut under the frame         | Smothered inside hooks                       |
+| Active Hands      | Feint-to-hook, body shots, layered combinations                         | Single predictable jab or cross              |
+
+## 6. Best guards against each punch
+
+| Incoming Punch       | Best Guard Responses                                      | Weaker Guard Responses                                  |
+| -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
+| Jab (head)           | High Guard, Long Guard, Active Hands, Peek-a-Boo          | Low Lead Hand if reflexes late; Philly Shell (lead low) |
+| Cross / straight     | High Guard, Philly Shell (roll), Cross-Arm                | Low or open guards                                      |
+| Lead hook (head)     | High Guard, Ear-Muff, Peek-a-Boo                          | Philly Shell with misaligned shoulder                   |
+| Rear hook (head)     | High Guard, Ear-Muff, Cross-Arm                           | Long Guard once opponent is inside                      |
+| Uppercut             | Tight Elbow Guard, Peek-a-Boo, Cross-Arm (low stack)      | Loose High Guard, Ear-Muff, Cross-Arm (high stack)      |
+| Body hook            | Tight Elbow Guard, Peek-a-Boo, elbows-tucked standard     | Long Guard, Low Lead Hand, Ear-Muff                     |
+| Body jab / straight  | Tight Elbow Guard, elbow drop, active parry               | Ear-Muff, loose High Guard                              |
+| Overhand             | High Guard, Ear-Muff, Cross-Arm, Philly Shell (roll)      | Long Guard, Low Lead Hand                               |
+| Flurry / combination | Peek-a-Boo, Ear-Muff, Cross-Arm                           | Active Hands when overwhelmed; Shell vs volume          |
+| Feint-to-punch       | Disciplined High Guard, compact guards, patience          | Active Hands, Low Lead Hand, overreactive shell         |
+
+---
+
+## 7. General punch effectiveness (base tuning guidance)
+
+**Highest percentage.** The jab is the most important punch in boxing — safest, most thrown, sets up everything; it should carry the highest base value in AI decision weighting. The cross is the most reliable power shot. The lead hook is the mid-range knockout punch of choice because it arrives from outside the eyeline. **Body shots deserve systemic strength: no guard covers head and body simultaneously, so investment downstairs pays off against nearly every defense.** This should fall out of the guard model above as an emergent truth, not a hardcoded buff — if it doesn't, the zone coverage values are wrong.
+
+**Situational.** Uppercuts are devastating at close range with a specific opening, and badly exposing when thrown from distance. The overhand is high-risk, high-reward.
+
+**Low percentage.** Wide haymakers, bolo punches, lead uppercuts from range, and any arm punch thrown from outside one's reach.
+
+## 8. Implementation notes
+
+Guard choice should modify more than damage taken:
+
+```text
+damage_reduction (per zone)
+stamina_cost
+visibility
+counter_readiness
+movement_speed
+ring_position_loss
+guard_integrity
+arm_condition_damage
+counter_window_access
+offense_modifiers      # per-punch speed/availability from inside the guard:
+                       #   cross-arm slows straights, shell slows the power jab,
+                       #   ear-muff disables offense, low lead hides lead-side shots
+guard_state            # cross-arm low/high stack; shell aligned/misaligned shoulder
+attribute_scaling      # peek-a-boo <-> conditioning + head movement
+                       # low lead   <-> reflexes/timing
+                       # shell      <-> ringcraft
+stance_interaction     # shell penalty vs southpaw opponents
+```
+
+**Decay hooks.** Peek-a-boo degrades *within* a fight as stamina drains; the low lead degrades *across a career* as reflexes age. Both map cleanly onto in-fight stamina and career-aging systems.
+
+**CPU reasoning from observable evidence** (knowledge should be functional, not decorative):
+
+```text
+Opponent holding high shell repeatedly.
+Recent head jabs blocked. Body lane open.
+Uppercut intercept risk depends on elbow position.
+-> switch to body jab, body hook, feint-to-uppercut, or angle step.
+```
+
+```text
+Opponent using long guard.
+Outside entries failing.
+-> try overhand over the frame, step-inside body hook, or slip past the lead hand.
+```
+
+```text
+Self currently in cross-arm guard.
+Fast 1-2 is speed-penalized from this structure.
+-> prefer clubbing hook / overhand, or transition guard before opening up.
+```
+
+## 9. Source-conflict log (editorial decisions)
+
+The three research passes disagreed on three points. Resolutions recorded here so future edits don't silently flip a deliberate call.
+
+1. **Cross-arm vs uppercut.** One pass claimed the stacked forearms completely block the center channel (strong vs uppercut); the other two said uppercuts split the cross-arm. Both are true at different arm heights, so it is modeled as a two-state guard (§3.4): the low stack bars the upward channel but opens the overhand; the high stack covers the overhand but is splittable underneath. Richer gameplay, and it matches film.
+2. **Long guard vs overhand.** One pass listed overhands as a long-guard strength; the other two — and boxing convention, where the overhand over the pawing lead is the canonical counter — list it as the guard's signature vulnerability. Resolved: vulnerability (§3.5).
+3. **Lead uppercut from the Philly shell.** Listed as mechanically awkward in one pass, yet elite shell users (Mayweather, Toney) landed it routinely. Resolved: available but speed/skill-penalized rather than absent (§3.3).
+
+## 10. Commentary and flavor hooks
+
+- Guard-origin line (§2): gloves are what made guards possible.
+- Exemplar name-drops per guard (§3) for style-identification commentary.
+- Canonical upset beat: Douglas timing Tyson's peek-a-boo entries with the jab-and-uppercut — the template for "the system got solved" fight narratives.
+- Closing philosophy line: *every guard is a trade — protection in one zone is sold from another. Elite defense is head movement, footwork, and distance; the guard is only the last line.*
